WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.413
NIST Special Publication 800-82 Revision 3 is a guide to operational technology security.

00:00:07.413 --> 00:00:19.939
It helps organizations apply cybersecurity practices to systems that monitor or control physical processes while accounting for performance, reliability, availability, and safety requirements.

00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:23.773
Operational technology is a broad category.

00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:38.344
It includes industrial control systems, supervisory control and data acquisition systems, distributed control systems, programmable logic controllers, building automation, transportation systems, physical-access controls, and other cyber-physical technologies.

00:00:38.344 --> 00:00:45.246
The common feature is that digital functions observe or influence real-world operations.

00:00:45.246 --> 00:00:48.569
This connection changes security priorities.

00:00:48.569 --> 00:00:54.960
A compromised business system may expose information or interrupt a service.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:05.441
An OT incident may also stop production, damage equipment, impair operator visibility, affect the environment, or create unsafe conditions.

00:01:05.441 --> 00:01:11.831
Security controls must reduce cyber risk without introducing unacceptable operational risk.

00:01:11.831 --> 00:01:22.312
The publication provides practical guidance on architectures, threats, vulnerabilities, risk management, security controls, monitoring, incident response, and ongoing maintenance.

00:01:22.312 --> 00:01:32.281
It supports engineering, operations, cybersecurity, risk, and leadership teams that need a shared approach to protecting operational systems.

00:01:32.281 --> 00:01:38.672
NIST SP 800-82 is guidance, not a replacement for site-specific engineering.

00:01:38.672 --> 00:01:45.063
Every facility has different processes, dependencies, safety safeguards, vendors, and constraints.

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:55.543
Use the guide to structure analysis and control selection, then validate decisions against the actual architecture and operating conditions.
